+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Register localization (--local-regs)
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Every lifted statement reads and writes the guest registers in ctx. Guest
+# memory goes through char pointers (vm_base), so after ctx has been handed to
+# any call the compiler cannot prove a guest store does not reach it: each
+# register value reaches its next use through memory, and dead register stores
+# cannot be dropped. With --local-regs, a function keeps GPRs, CR, XER, LR and
+# CTR in C locals and syncs them with ctx only where something else can look
+# at ctx:
+#   - a "sync" line (a call to a lifted function, an indirect call, a syscall,
+#     an HLE call, anything else handed ctx) runs unchanged against ctx: the
+#     locals that may be dirty are written back before it, and the locals that
+#     are live after it are reloaded;
+#   - an exit (return, trampoline hand-off, the default arm of a jump table)
+#     runs unchanged against ctx after a write-back of the dirty locals.
+# Dirty = may have been written since the last write-back or reload (forward
+# dataflow over the line CFG); live = may be read before the next definite
+# write (backward dataflow, a write-back counting as a read). Clang turns the
+# locals into SSA values (their address only reaches always-inline helpers), so
+# guest memory accesses no longer pin them. FPRs, VRs, FPSCR, VSCR and the
+# reservation stay in ctx. A function with a line shape the pass does not
+# recognise is emitted unchanged (counted in LOCAL_REGS_STATS).
